<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 3 – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Much Is the Pineapple?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Describe quantities and dates using numbers up to a hundred. interactive: Participate in role-plays based on classroom scenarios.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe the quantity of items needed using numbers (e.g., 'I need five...'). Can participate in role-plays by responding to questions about items and prices in a market setting (e.g., 'How many apples do you need?' 'Three apples, please.'). Can ask for prices using correct structures, for example, using simple WH-questions and phrases in a role-play (e.g., 'How much is the pineapple?' 'It is one dollar.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays large flashcards or realia of various fruits and vegetables (pineapple, cassava, potatoes, apples, bananas) on the board or desk. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and encourages students to name the items. Then, the teacher says, "Let's go shopping at the market!" and leads a short chant related to market items and numbers (e.g., "One pineapple, two potatoes, three apples, let's go!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a pineapple and models the target phrases: "How much is the pineapple? It is one dollar." The teacher then holds up a picture of five apples and models, "I need five apples. They are two dollars." The teacher demonstrates polite expressions like 'Excuse me, how much is it?' and 'Three apples, please.' The teacher exaggerates the /p/ sound in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money' and asks students to repeat. The teacher also shows how to count numbers from 1 to 20 using fingers or number cards, focusing on blending sounds for words like 'one' and 'twenty'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I want one pineapple, do I say 'I need one pineapple?'" (Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I say 'How much it is?' or 'How much is it?'" (How much is it?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I ask for something, do I say 'please'?" (Yes)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated dialogue script presented on a poster or projector, featuring two characters (Customer and Vendor) at a market stall with images of various items (pineapple, potatoes, cassava, apples, bananas) and their prices (using numbers 1-20 initially).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can ask and give basic information about items and prices in a market setting, using simple WH-questions and quantities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Customer:</b> "Excuse me! Good morning!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Vendor:</b> "Good morning! How can I help you?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Customer:</b> "How much is the pineapple?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Vendor:</b> "It is one dollar."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Customer:</b> "Okay. I want three apples and two bananas, please."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Vendor:</b> "Three apples and two bananas. That is four dollars."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Customer:</b> "Thank you! Here is the money."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Vendor:</b> "Thank you! Have a nice day!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Where are the two people?" (At the market) "Who wants to buy something?" (The customer)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The pineapple costs five dollars." (False) "The customer buys bananas." (True)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the item picture to its price from the dialogue.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice asking and responding to prices ('How much is it/are they?'), describing quantities ('I need/want [number] [item]'), and using numbers 1-100. Focus on the Present Simple, polite expressions, and clear pronunciation of /p/ and /m/ sounds, as well as number blending (e.g., 'twenty').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Fill and Speak":</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with pictures of different market items (e.g., 5 potatoes, 1 pineapple, 10 bananas) and blank spaces for quantity and price. Students fill in the blanks using numbers and then read the sentences aloud. Example: "I need ___ potatoes. They are ___ dollars." and "How much is the ___? It is ___ dollar." The teacher monitors for correct use of numbers (1-100) and pronunciation, especially for /p/ in 'potatoes' and 'pineapple'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Chain Shopping":</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by saying, "I want two apples." The next student adds an item and quantity, "I want two apples and three pineapples." The chain continues, with each student repeating the previous items and adding a new one, using numbers up to 20-30. The teacher ensures correct 'and' as a discourse marker and checks for clear pronunciation of /p/ and /m/ sounds and number blending.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Price Tag Match":</b> In pairs, students receive a set of flashcards with market items (pineapple, cassava, potatoes, money, etc.) and separate cards with prices (e.g., "1 dollar," "5 dollars," "25 dollars"). Student A picks an item card, for example, a picture of 'cassava', and asks Student B, "How much is the cassava?" Student B picks a price card and responds, "It is [number] dollars." They swap roles. The teacher circulates, providing support on 'How much is it?' and 'It is...' structures, and reinforcing polite language.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired role-play: "Shopping at the Market."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Task Description:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two roles: "Customer" and "Vendor." The Customer receives a "shopping list" with 3-4 items and quantities (e.g., "I need 2 pineapples, 4 potatoes, 10 apples"). The Vendor receives a "price list" for these items (e.g., Pineapple: $1, Potatoes: $0.50 each, Apples: $0.25 each). Students use classroom props (flashcards, realia, pretend money) to set up a simple market stall. They then perform a short dialogue where the Customer asks for items and prices, and the Vendor responds. Students must use polite expressions like "Excuse me," "please," and "thank you." They are encouraged to use numbers up to 100 in their interactions, describing quantities (e.g., 'I need twenty-five apples.') and asking/stating prices (e.g., 'It is ninety dollars.'). The teacher observes and encourages fluency and spontaneous interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Interaction:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Customer:</b> "Excuse me! Good afternoon."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vendor:</b> "Good afternoon! How can I help you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Customer:</b> "How much is the pineapple?" (Focus on /p/)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vendor:</b> "It is one dollar."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Customer:</b> "Okay, I need four potatoes and seven apples, please."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vendor:</b> "Four potatoes and seven apples. That is three dollars. Here is your money." (Focus on /m/)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Customer:</b> "Thank you very much!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Shopping at the Market" role-play in pairs.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> (e.g., clear /p/ in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money', blending of numbers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., market, price, pineapple, potatoes, money, buy, sell, need, want).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> (e.g., WH-questions like 'How much is it?', quantities like 'I need five...', Present Simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and fluency:</b> Students' ability to communicate effectively and participate in the role-play without excessive pauses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Students engage with their partner to complete the task.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of polite expressions:</b> (e.g., 'Excuse me', 'please', 'thank you').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection form:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask for prices using 'How much is it?' (Yes / No)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can say what I need using 'I need...' and numbers up to 100. (Yes / No)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I used polite words like 'please' and 'thank you' in my role-play. (Yes / No)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when shopping at the market with my partner?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult or what do I need more practice with?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Today, students actively engaged in role-plays, demonstrating good use of 'How much is it?' and 'I need...' structures. The "Chain Shopping" activity was particularly effective for reinforcing numbers and vocabulary. Some students still need more practice with numbers above 50 and consistent use of polite expressions. The pronunciation of /p/ and /m/ was generally good, but some still struggle with blending sounds for specific numbers. I need to provide more explicit modeling and drill for higher numbers and encourage more natural interaction by providing less structured prompts next time.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing), students will apply the vocabulary and structures learned today by writing their own shopping lists and short market dialogues, focusing on accurate spelling of numbers and items. This will consolidate their understanding and prepare them for more independent language production.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>